    "result_text": "The full test suite is green \u2014 **71 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS** \u2014 and all tests live in the `acceptance` package, so nothing was skipped.\n\n## Result\n\nThe specification is fully satisfied. All rejection responses in the 400/409/429 range flow through a single `@ControllerAdvice` (`ExceptionControllerAdvice`) whose handlers each build their body via `detailBuild(...)`, producing a Spring `ProblemDetail`:\n\n- **`type`** \u2014 `problemDetail.setType(...)` (request URL as URI)\n- **`title`** \u2014 `problemDetail.setTitle(...)` (exception class simple name)\n- **`status`** \u2014 `ProblemDetail.forStatus(status)` (matches the HTTP status, unchanged)\n- **`detail`** \u2014 `problemDetail.setDetail(...)`\n\nReturning `ProblemDetail` makes Spring serialize it as `application/problem+json`. I verified this end-to-end:\n\n- **400** paths: `MethodArgumentNotValidException`, `MissingOwnerFieldsException`, `InvalidTelephoneException`, `InvalidEmailException`, `InvalidPostcodeException`, `FutureRegistrationDateException`\n- **409** paths: `DuplicateTelephoneException`, `DuplicateEmailException`, `DuplicateHouseholdException`, `CityAtCapacityException`\n- **429** path: `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException`\n\nA grep of `src/main` confirmed there are **no other rejection paths** (no ad-hoc `BAD_REQUEST`/`CONFLICT`/`TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`/`ResponseStatusException`) that could bypass the advice.\n\nThe new acceptance test `Cp59Tests` \u2014 which asserts a 400 rejection returns `content-type` compatible with `application/problem+json` and a JSON `$.status` of `400` \u2014 passes, as does every existing test. The change is already in place and the suite is green; no further code modifications were required.",
